[2026-09-30T20:51:58.572Z] INFO faser.utils_med: As a lone rescuer performing chest compressions on an infant, you would encircle the infant's chest and give 30 compressions just below the nipple line using two fingers, ensuring a rate of 100 to 120 compressions per minute and a depth of about 1 1/2 inches or 1/3 of the chest depth. After compressions, if necessary, you would pinch the infant's nose, seal your mouth around the child's mouth, and give 2 rescue breaths, making sure the chest rises visibly. If this does not occur, you should continue with 30 compressions followed by 2 rescue breaths and consider leaving the area briefly to call for emergency assistance if needed.
